// The one place a device-visible address leaves a DMA span (GitHub issues
// #1162, #637 stage 3). A device span -- from a dma_fixed allocation or a
// device protocol owner's `_span` -- carries an address the type system
// proved inside its allocation; turning it into a number a device (or a
// CPU-side cache operation) is given is the trusted step, and it is taken
// only here. This file is a raw mint file (kernel/RAW_MINT_FILES); its
// callers are fixed by scripts/allowed_callers.tsv.
//
// The rule, reviewed once: a span's CPU physical address is
// dma_span_address(span) (the kernel maps RAM at identity); a device sees
// that address plus the inbound offset of the bus it sits behind --
//   - QEMU virt's virtio-mmio devices: offset 0 (virtio_span_address);
//   - RP1's devices (GEM, xHCI) behind the RPi5 PCIe root complex:
//     rp1_dma_pci_offset, validated from the boot DTB's dma-ranges
//     (pcie_address_map_dtb.tkb) (rp1_span_bus_address);
//   - a CPU-side cache or DDB read of the span: no offset
//     (dma_span_cpu_address).
// Trusted, not checked: that each driver names the function of its own bus,
// and that a device given the address touches only the span's bytes. The
// PCIe inbound window (#1032, pcie2_dma_inbound_setup) bounds that second
// trust for RP1's devices to the one RP1 DMA arena, in hardware; within the
// arena it stays trusted. QEMU's virtio-mmio devices are not confined yet.

// A device span's address for a QEMU virt virtio-mmio queue or descriptor:
// QEMU virt's DMA addresses are physical, with no bus offset.
fn virtio_span_address(span: borrow DmaDeviceSpan) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { dma_span_address(span) };
}

// A device span's RP1 bus address, for a GEM or xHCI descriptor or register.
fn rp1_span_bus_address(span: borrow DmaDeviceSpan) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { dma_span_address(span) } + boot_once_get(&rp1_dma_pci_offset);
}

// A device span's CPU physical address, for a CPU-side cache operation on it.
fn dma_span_cpu_address(span: borrow DmaDeviceSpan) -> usize !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { dma_span_address(span) };
}
